{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1539161-spirit-bans-and-folk-horror","id":1539161,"title":"Spirit Bans and Folk Horror","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-07-24T13:19:33-07:00","id":1539161,"title":"Spirit Bans and Folk Horror","content":"<br /> <b>I like adding a bit of \u2018cult-to-the-thousand-legged-goat-of-the-woods\u2019 \u2018bust-of-the-great-god-pan\u2019, \u2018the-white-people-who-live-beneath-the-mounds\u2019 sort of type flavor to Garou. I think higher Gnosis doesn\u2019t have a listed downside because the difficulties it creates are too idiosyncratic compared to the struggles of high rage, but that there is a similar distinction from human thinking that emerges.</b><br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Also I love the idea that with their tales and pacts and bans and geasa that the Garou are themselves, even the most Wyld-influenced of them, guilty of mirroring the Weavers\u2019 sin. Creating webs of legends and definition that drags the empirical to the meaningful and the abstract to the concrete, the fleeting to permanent.</b><br /> <br /> <br /> <b>So what are some spirit bans or or traditions that you could give garou or kin (kenning or otherwise) that helps to give that fun Folk Horror feeling?</b><ul><li><b>In a Silver Fang Sept the enclave of a couple Children of Gaia and their kin must hang a small (lowercase f) fetish of a falcons feather and horsehair or goat-horn in any permanent residence. Those who dwell within are required to toast to the sun, \u2018from gleaming to twilight\u2019 before drinking alcohol or fresh water. An unkenning PC or NPC might not know why it is a big deal to knock down the 20 year old dusty handicraft of fraying old wolfhair and a sunbleached feather, but the kinfolk who live there are freaking out or the garou checking in on them may be making a rage roll.</b></li> <li><b>A glasswalker bawn requires that one person at every party or rite or event held near the caern wears an Iron horseshoe prominently. They don\u2019t even remember why as the story is lost but it is known that it offends the spirits on account of some old Iron Rider or Warder pact if the horseshoe is concealed, or worse, not worn.</b></li> <li><b>Maybe that same glasswalker sept insists on arranged marriages for anyone who takes up residence with them. Even if it isn\u2019t recognized outside the caern, you cannot dwell within unless you have agreed to be part of \u2018the family tree\u2019 which is encoded in a bunch of old nixie tube, rotary dial, relay based calculator. Or more plainly just hand matched pairs of people candles of cynocephalic saints without explaining why.</b></li> <li><b>A Fenrir bawn or kinfolk enclave where it is important that each home have their own homebrew bear or mead or liquor which is then mixed with blood and poured on the ground as an apotropaic when a bad omen is witnessed.</b></li> <li><b>A group of Fenrir where they and their kinfolk are required to hang a bone from the first thing they\u2019ve killed and they traditionally add it to a windchime or the like near the house. An expansion of that where the midwife who works with the Fenrir, and maybe occasionally offers her service to rough and tumble people outside the nation who don\u2019t offend them or who have cash, will bring a mouse or a worm or a half dead rat or bird and doesn\u2019t consider the birth to be complete until the infant has killed some small animal prepared for that purpose.</b></li> <li><b>A family of bone gnawer kin who have a tradition that they have to at least pretend to eat or drink a little of something before throwing it away, even if the food or drink is spoiled. Or they aren\u2019t allowed to throw away drink-can tabs and must collect them for as long as they stay in one place.</b></li> <li><b>Silent Strider residences might be marked by having the wings of a dead crow somewhere on the premises or by having an eye carved into the wall. The family goes up on the roof at sunset on every full moon to listen to the prevailing wind (Imagine seeing a family of four who just moved in, every intentionally setting up a ladder, carefully guiding the young children \u2018safely\u2019 up onto the roof, and then the four of them stand, silent, listening to the wind for an hour as the sun goes down. You ask them about it and they just say, \u201csometimes you can hear wisdom on the wind.\u201d \u201cLike a mindfulness thing?\u201d \u201cSort of\u2026\u201d)</b></li> <li><b>Fish pies and Wassailing are a goto at a Fianna camp which may also feature an abundance of shed horn decoration and garb. Meat that\u2019s prepared has to be touched to deerhorn, or the person to cook it has to jump over the raw meat before it is prepared.</b></li> <li><b>A camp of black fury kin requires men to wear a sprig of mistletoe somewhere on their person that, when they offend a woman they have to throw on the ground to be stepped on. </b></li> <li><b>A church in Black Fury territory which has taken on pentecostal like traditions of speaking in gibberish or practicing communal echolalia (to attack the meanings of words and the patriarch)</b></li> <li><b>An otherwise normal suburban house that has to have a human skeleton hidden somewhere within. A ban on a set of families of Shadow Lord kin that parallels the ODD Thomas society and is symbolic of their methods of keeping secrets.</b></li> <li><b>Unkenning Child of Gaia kinfolk who nonetheless make their children sit inside a circle while the family dances around them with smoking sprigs of sage and willow (a misinterpreted rite of cleansing that no one has seen fit to correct.)</b></li> </ul><br /> <br /> <b>Just things that might remind Garou players that they are not fully human and that even their human families and associates are touched by that little strand of uncanniness that would make human PCs or NPCs alert that they are in a strange place with unpredictable habits or traditions. Odd additions to saying grace or responses to sneezes or stomach grumblings.</b><br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Like how the mayor's family always had arrowroot or had strange sigils carved into the mantlestone or hearthstone in Sleepy Hollow, or the animal masks and talismans and odd ribbons and Gardens in Wicker Man. Animals not typically thought of as pets being treated with an odd familiarity or affection or even reverence. Or alternatively animals treated with strange contempt or even venom (Owls brood resent crows and prepare mice in ways that might appear torturous. Striders or Silver fangs may make a show of dead snakes found in their territory as a mark of loyalty to Falcon or of venom against Apep/Set, Gnawers keeping bandoleers of cat tails, etc.).</b><ul><li><b>Go into a Glasswalker kin\u2019s house and there are spiders in every corner, their presence not only permitted, but seemingly encouraged, as an innocent eyed dark-haired child dutifully gathers up still living flies and moths from the windows and with the domestic air of a chore deposits the insects in each web through the house.</b></li> <li><b>Entering into the squat, overburdened townhouse of a Bone Gnawer there is a clatter from what was a kitchen as the half dozen rodents foraging therein flinch, but as they see the Gnawer kin resident walk in behind you they relax and resume their foraging, seeming to almost ignore the human presence.</b></li> <li><b>Chicken bones and bits of rawhide are strewn between the trailers of the Fenrir mobile home park, arranged into spirals and spiderwebs by the crows and ravens and magpies that flit to and fro within. Their mess and cacophony effortlessly tuned out by the residents, or at least the ones that know what is good for them. Chickens are kept almost for this purpose and seem to be treated with great contempt, some chickens slaughtered for food may be left out for the corvids, uneaten, if they did not provide a \u2018good struggle\u2019 at the end.</b></li> <li><b>Any number of culinary oddities that might follow from the family member with rage who check in on you from time to time having a connection to the spirits that themselves may remember a bygone human culture that you were never a part of.</b></li> </ul><br /> <br /> <b>Just really emphasize the inhuman aspect by highlighting the \u2018how strange this might look to an outsider who accidentally saw a little too much\u2019.<br /> <br /> Any other ideas for ominous weirdness that could follow Kenning/Unkenning Kin and Garou?</b><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27296-devidramoth","name":"Devidramoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27296_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"54"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-24T14:17:04-07:00","id":1539164,"title":"","content":"Overall I like the idea of those families having those weird habits they inherited from the Garou, as a show of how the Shapeshifters distinguish themselves culturally from the neighbors.<br /> <br /> But the way you describe this as &quot;inhuman&quot;... I don't think this is the better phrasing for that. This kind of traditions and rites exist all over the world, among all kinds of people.<br /> <br /> Lovecraft described those kinds of rites as alien because he was racist and did, indeed, saw cultures diverse from his own as inhuman in the worst sense.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-24T21:04:59-07:00","id":1539184,"title":"","content":"The Garou are not human is what I was trying to get at, and in a very real sense kin aren't fully human either and even if they were they have to cater to the needs and considerations of things that aren't human. While they would parallel real world traditions and to some extent I am, no doubt, incidentally drawing inspiration from such things, but what I'm aiming for is the uncanniness. <br /> <br /> To give an anecdote, I was once travelling and got a little lost somewhere in the valleys between West Virginia and Maryland, and as I came to a place with two roads coming together there was a barbeque place and a gas station. So I figured I'd get a bite to eat then hop over to the gas station and pick up a map to try and figure out a way back to the highway. When I walked into the barbeque place it felt like everyone in there turned to look at me, and as I went to take a seat I noticed that everyone I could lay my eyes on was wearing something with pigs on it, a piggy t-shirt, plastic pig ring, pink piggy earrings, just everywhere. I thought maybe it was just one family but looking around these people weren't together, didn't look similar but all had decided to wear pig memorabilia and all seemed leery of me, an outsider, being there. I took the BBQ to go and it was quite good. <br /> <br /> Obviously those were just people. Obviously they had their own reasons for wearing what they were wearing, something mundane no doubt. Maybe they were staring at me, in which case they had their reasons, no doubt. Or maybe I was just feeling out of place while lost and was unusually sensitive to glances, it's happened before. But imagine if it weren't.<br /> <br /> That's what I'm aiming for, what if the thing that feels sinister in real life because your brain is being unreasonable, actually is, if not sinister, actually numenous in the WoD? I don't think it's racist, but I suppose that it is good to be sensitive to such things.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27296-devidramoth","name":"Devidramoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27296_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"54"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-24T21:46:10-07:00","id":1539185,"title":"","content":"These are good and very much the things I add into my depictions of garou and kin.<br /> <br /> But I think you should check out animism and traditions tied to it before calling these things inhuman. I grew up collecting flowers and watching a pyre each midsummer, I melted tin to see the future during the new year and the first l\u00f6yly in a sauna was for the sauna elf.<br /> <br /> So I would treat these less with horror and more with 'this is how the garou in this region have seen the tribal ban and how it applies to their kin.' For example, I have a headcanon that particularly devout garou will follow their tribal patron's ban even if the patron isn't their pack patron.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://ana-mizuki.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My gallery.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83e\udd70","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/110-ana-mizuki","name":"Ana Mizuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar110_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2484"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-25T13:49:45-07:00","id":1539243,"title":"","content":"<br /> Thank you for your words of caution. My intention is to create fictional traditions connected to non-human shapeshifters who walk and talk among humans. These traditions may resemble actual human customs to evoke an uncanny feeling for storytellers and players. However, I realize I did not adequately consider how my portrayal could inadvertently alienate real people.<br /> <br /> Could you suggest a better way to describe the sense of dread one might feel seeing a mother hand a child a small shuffling parcel, only for that child to later sneak into the woods at twilight to tie a living mouse among the trees as the eyes of half a dozen owls silently watch? I understand that &quot;inhuman&quot; is problematic, as it could harm real people who resemble these fictional characters and their families.<br /> <br /> I'm looking for a term to convey the uncanny feeling of encountering cultural norms and mores that are suddenly different from what you expected, akin to stepping off a sandbar into unexpectedly deep water or struggling to say grace in a foreign language at a friend's house. This sense of disorientation and unease is intensified when it involves non-human influences, like spirits or murderous shapeshifters.<br /> <br /> What would you call this heightened sense of uncanniness that comes from realizing the presence of these otherworldly forces?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27296-devidramoth","name":"Devidramoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27296_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"54"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-25T14:43:16-07:00","id":1539249,"title":"","content":"I think &quot;uncanny&quot; is a good start, it's just &quot;inhuman&quot; that's more of a problem. Nothing against the idea that Garou and Kinfolk aren't human, either, it's just in this context that it is a problem.<br /> <br /> To be honest I can't think of anything better than &quot;uncanny&quot; right now. For a longer description, it is the eerie sensation you have when you're suddenly in front of a social rite you never expected.<br /> <br /> Which is, again, very in tune with Kinfolk. After all, what's really uncanny isn't that they have such rites and beliefs, but that it is something particular to them and alien to the greater cultural environment they're in.<br /> <br /> But yeah, real people also use those shoes. Some of the players that use this trope are sometimes on the uncanny side of things irl, like myself.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-25T17:10:23-07:00","id":1539271,"title":"","content":"A lot of the chiminages I use are tailored to local folklore and beliefs it really helps to root your Chronicle into a location and its history.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/479-damian-may","name":"Damian May","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar479_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-25T20:22:18-07:00","id":1539289,"title":"","content":"I feel you are approaching this from the wrong angle. It isn't that these traditions can't be uncanny, but the reason they are uncanny is that they harken back to cultures and traditions that existed before Christianity came to their lands.<br /> <br /> Aside from a few exceptions, garou generally pay lip service to Abrahamic faiths and still follow the ways of their ancestors they apply to the modern world. Yes, they might not be able to find a branch untouched by humans, but the cleansing rite still works with an incense stick and a water bottle.<br /> <br /> For garou who are part of the local Indigenous cultures, they generally slot the garou beliefs in the already animistic worldview. In countries like Japan that have animistic faith as part of their heritage, the uncanny comes not from the practises themselves being odd but the extreme nature of them (see. the villages in the Fatal Frame games.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://ana-mizuki.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My gallery.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/110-ana-mizuki","name":"Ana Mizuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar110_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2484"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-29T15:20:14-07:00","id":1539503,"title":"","content":"I wanna start by saying that I really liked this thread Devidramath and if you ever have any further ideas or examples in this vein I\u2019d encourage you to post them, this really got my mind going!<br /> <br /> I really like the concept you mention early in your post about how, via the pacts that they uphold and the various chiminage and relations they maintain with spiritual beings, Garou create a sort of umbral superstructure for themselves that they must replicate and reproduce; I really like that way of imagining it and imo the tensions which arise from the conflicts both internal to and external to that umbral-superstructure are really juicy for storytelling in WtA.<br /> <br /> I also like the way that many of these examples could be used to clearly demonstrate something about particular social groups and communities of Garou &amp; kin and how their historical context and relations can be shown via their umbral-superstructure. That to me keys into one of my philosophical tenets of Gothic storytelling that the crimes of the past (and the past/history in general) are perpetually recurring in the Gothic mode and these spiritual bans, just as clearly as a wraith\u2019s haunting or the temporally-displaced vampire could, depict how the past can intrude upon the present in the gothic mode.<br /> <br /> I also think that other posters on this thread have raised valuable points about Folk Horror that I myself am wrestling with the aspects of. There are some critics who make the claim that horror as a genre is inherently reactionary in its politics/ethics and while I don\u2019t agree with that position, I think that it does speak to a common tendency of the social (re)production of horror narratives. The philosophy of this is something I\u2019m still tangling with but it feels connected with the currents raised by this thread.<br /> <br /> If I come up with any particular examples later on I\u2019ll try to add them but for the time being I simply want to say that I find the examples provided already in this thread to be very provocative and that the various ideas that this thread touches on are valuable for a storyteller and writer\u2019s consideration as their planning their chronicle.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}